Polling confirms the damage, with caveats. Datafolha, which completed fieldwork before the audio broke, showed Lula and Flávio tied at 45% — confirming the prior dead-heat reading. AtlasIntel's daily tracker, run after the scandal, showed Lula at 49.1% and Flávio at 42.6%. AtlasIntel is volatile, and whether the lead holds beyond the immediate news cycle is the key question for the next polling cycle. What the numbers cannot obscure is the structural condition underneath: Datafolha registered government approval at 39% negative and 30% positive. Lula's personal approval sits at 45% — above his government's performance rating and now above his opponent. The lead is scandal-dependent, not performance-driven.

That structural vulnerability is about to be tested in the most direct way possible. Petrobras reported record first-quarter results this week — R$32.7 billion in net profit, up 110% quarter on quarter, production at 3.23 million barrels of oil equivalent per day, the company ranked the world's most profitable oil major. The same company is absorbing roughly US$1 billion in losses on subsidised gasoline, held below international parity as oil prices rose on the US-Iran conflict. A price increase exceeding 15% is anticipated. Five months before the first round, the government faces a blunt choice: pass the cost to consumers and absorb the political anger, or continue absorbing losses at a company already carrying the weight of industrial policy, regional investment, and pre-electoral positioning simultaneously. Decree 12.974, issued this week, tightens the enforcement of diesel subsidies — requiring documentary proof of pass-through from importers to retailers, with federal verification and sanctions for non-compliance by May 31. The message it sends is precise and politically awkward: truckers and agribusiness are protected; every other driver is not.

The week also produced Decree 12.966, establishing a federal programme to coordinate intelligence, investigative, and operational capabilities against organised crime and, explicitly, private militias. The decree landed in the same week that connections between the Bolsonaro family and Rio's militia networks were dominating headlines — context that muddies whether this is structural security policy or well-timed political signalling. The militias are a real governance problem: they control over 20% of Rio de Janeiro's territory, and their role in the assassination of Marielle Franco was confirmed by conviction in February. The decree names them as a target. Whether it generates operational activity or serves primarily as a message during a week of maximum political pressure on a family with documented militia ties requires watching.

On the diplomatic track, Poder360 reported that Lula made three separate requests to the American president for help extraditing Ricardo Magro, owner of the Refit company and subject of a Supreme Court arrest order and R$5.2 billion asset freeze. The requests are outside the formal organised crime working group established at the May 7 Lula-Trump meeting and predate the new institutional framework. The 30-day tariff working group from that meeting has produced no announced outcomes — but the window does not close until around June 6, so the absence is not yet a signal.

Two things remain conspicuously unresolved. The Superior Military Court has yet to rule on stripping rank from generals convicted over January 8 — a request pending since February 3. The Supreme Court's full review of the suspended sentencing law for the January 8 convicts has not been scheduled, even as a Quaest poll found 52% of Brazilians oppose any sentence reduction. Both are decisions the government would prefer resolved before the campaign fully ignites. Each additional week without a ruling adds to the wait.",
